      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "Retrospective study of clinical outcomes of 1-staged combined cervical and lumbar decompression for patients with tandem spinal stenosis (TSS)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To describe middle-term clinical outcomes of this procedure."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "SUMMARY AND BACKGROUND DATA",
          "content": "Little is known with regard to the clinical outcomes of 1-staged combined cervical and lumbar decompression for TSS."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "Surgical intervention, perioperative complications, and clinical outcomes were reviewed in 17 TSS patients who underwent 1-staged combined cervical and lumbar decompression and were followed-up for more than 3 years. Clinical symptoms were evaluated using the Japan Orthopaedic Association Score for back pain (JOA-B) and cervical myelopathy (JOA-C) and activity of daily life, before surgery, at 6 months postoperatively, and at final follow-up. Patient satisfaction was determined at final follow-up."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The JOA-B, JOA-C scores, and activities of daily life improved significantly 6 months after surgery, but ultimately deteriorated. At 6 months, the improvement ratios in JOA-B and JOA-C scores were positively correlated. Complications involving other parts of the body significantly influenced clinical deterioration. Twelve patients (71%) were satisfied."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "One-staged combined cervical and lumbar decompression for TSS provided fair results, even for elderly patients. Although reasons other than spinal pathology affected symptom deterioration at final follow-up, most patients expressed satisfaction at middle-term follow-up periods."
        }
      ]},
